public void setCamera(final ICamera cam) {
  if (this.camera() != null) {
    Game.loop().detach(camera);
  }

  camera = cam;

  if (cam != null && !Game.isInNoGUIMode()) {
    Game.loop().attach(cam);
    cam.updateFocus();
  }
}
```

The camera is set in at least two ways:

1. During game initialization:

```java
public static synchronized void init(String... args) {
  ...
  world.setCamera(new Camera());
  ...
}
```

This guarantees a non-null camera after `Game.init(...)` completes and before the usual game start flow, assuming no further modifications.

2. The camera can later be cleared:

```java
public void clear() {
  this.unloadEnvironment();
  this.environments.clear();
  this.setCamera(null);
  ...
}
```

So `setCamera(null)` will set `this.camera` to `null`.

There is no check preventing `setCamera(null)` from being called while the rest of the engine is still running. Multiple call sites explicitly perform null checks before using `Game.world().camera()`, for example:

```java
if (Game.world().camera() != null) {
  Game.world().camera().updateFocus();
}
```

and

```java
Rectangle2D viewport =
    Game.screens() != null && Game.world().camera() != null
        ? Game.world().camera().getViewport()
        : null;
```

These checks would be unnecessary if `Game.world().camera()` were guaranteed non-null.

Other code paths, however, use `Game.world().camera()` without any null check (e.g., `Environment.render`, `ColorLayer.render`, `SoundEngine.start`, many render helpers). That indicates a design assumption that a camera is *usually* present, but it is not enforced by the `GameWorld` API itself.

Because `clear()` calls `this.setCamera(null)`, and there is no shown invariant or guard that prevents `Game.world().camera()` from being called after `clear()`, the field `this.camera` can indeed be `null` at some execution times. Therefore, at the specific call site `Game.world().camera()`, the method can return `null` depending on the lifecycle state of the game world (e.g., after `clear()` has been invoked and before another non-null camera is set).

Hence, the invocation is not provably non-null and can yield `null`.
